exercises.solution:
```python
def first_conflict(path_a, path_b):
    def at(path, t):
        return path[min(t, len(path) - 1)]
    for t in range(max(len(path_a), len(path_b))):
        if at(path_a, t) == at(path_b, t):
            return ("vertex", t)
        if t > 0 and at(path_a, t) == at(path_b, t - 1) and at(path_b, t) == at(path_a, t - 1):
            return ("swap", t)
    return None
```

The starter misses two kinds of conflict. `zip` stops at the shorter path, but a robot that has finished still occupies its goal, so a later arrival there collides; and it never checks swaps, in which two robots pass *through* each other along an edge without ever sharing a cell at a whole time step. In conflict-based search, each conflict found this way becomes a branching point: a vertex conflict at cell $v$ and time $t$ creates two children, one forbidding robot A from $v$ at $t$ and one forbidding robot B; a swap conflict forbids one robot or the other from traversing that edge at that time.
